From: Generated patch
From: Arthur Vasseur <arthur@kyber.tech>
Date: Wed, 09 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avutil/hwcontext: fix bogus unmap detection in av_hwframe_map

av_hwframe_map() decides whether a call is really an "unmap" of a
previous mapping purely by comparing src/dst hw_frames_ctx and pixel
formats. That heuristic doesn't verify that src->buf[0] was actually
produced by a prior ff_hwframe_map_create() call, only that the formats
happen to line up.

This misfires for callers that build a sw->hw mapping "in reverse", such
as vf_hwmap's reverse mode: it attaches its own hwframes_ref to an
independently-allocated software frame before calling av_hwframe_map(),
purely so the format/context check above will match. When it does,
src->buf[0]->data is cast to a HWMapDescriptor* and its "source" field
is read as an AVFrame* to hand back via av_frame_replace() - but
buf[0]->data here is real pixel data from the software frame, not a
HWMapDescriptor. The "source" pointer read out of that pixel data is
effectively garbage, and dereferencing it crashes.

This was found with an XCB screen-grab source pushed through
"hwmap,scale_vaapi=format=nv12" into a VAAPI encoder on a discrete GPU
(Intel Arc): the software capture frame is independently allocated
(never obtained via hwmap's own get_buffer hook), so it reliably takes
this bogus path and segfaults on the very first frame.

Require src->buf[0]'s underlying AVBuffer to have been created by
ff_hwframe_map_create() (identified by its free callback being
ff_hwframe_unmap) before trusting it to hold a HWMapDescriptor. If it
isn't, fall through to the normal map_from/map_to path instead.
---
 libavutil/hwcontext.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/libavutil/hwcontext.c
+++ b/libavutil/hwcontext.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 
 #include "avassert.h"
 #include "buffer.h"
+#include "buffer_internal.h"
 #include "common.h"
 #include "hwcontext.h"
 #include "hwcontext_internal.h"
@@ -807,17 +808,30 @@
             (src_frames->source_frames &&
              src_frames->source_frames->data ==
              (uint8_t*)dst_frames)) {
-            // This is an unmap operation.  We don't need to directly
-            // do anything here other than fill in the original frame,
-            // because the real unmap will be invoked when the last
-            // reference to the mapped frame disappears.
+            // This looks like an unmap operation (src and dst agree on
+            // format/context), but that alone doesn't prove src actually
+            // came from a previous av_hwframe_map() call: a plain software
+            // frame that merely happens to have dst's hw_frames_ctx and
+            // sw_format attached (e.g. by a caller wanting a sw->hw map,
+            // such as vf_hwmap's reverse mode) matches the same condition.
+            // Require src->buf[0] to have actually been created by
+            // ff_hwframe_map_create() before trusting its data to be a
+            // HWMapDescriptor - otherwise buf[0]->data is plain frame data,
+            // and the "source" pointer read from it is garbage that
+            // av_frame_replace() would go on to dereference below.
             if (!src->buf[0]) {
                 av_log(src_frames, AV_LOG_ERROR, "Invalid mapping "
                        "found when attempting unmap.\n");
                 return AVERROR(EINVAL);
             }
-            hwmap = (HWMapDescriptor*)src->buf[0]->data;
-            return av_frame_replace(dst, hwmap->source);
+            if (src->buf[0]->buffer->free == &ff_hwframe_unmap) {
+                // This is an unmap operation.  We don't need to directly
+                // do anything here other than fill in the original frame,
+                // because the real unmap will be invoked when the last
+                // reference to the mapped frame disappears.
+                hwmap = (HWMapDescriptor*)src->buf[0]->data;
+                return av_frame_replace(dst, hwmap->source);
+            }
         }
     }
 
